ANOMALY DETECTION IN PREFERENCE DATA
============================================================

n = 6 products
E[H] = 22.5, CV = 0.577, sigma = 13.0

RANDOM (no structure):
  Mean H = 23.1, H/E[H] = 1.028
  Std H = 12.1
  Mean Z = 0.05

MANIPULATED (product 0 forced to beat 3,4,5):
  Mean H = 18.3, H/E[H] = 0.811
  Std H = 11.4
  Mean Z = -0.33

SKILL-BASED (real hierarchy, skills 3.0,2.5,2.0,1.5,1.0,0.5):
  Mean H = 15.7, H/E[H] = 0.699
  Std H = 11.0
  Mean Z = -0.52

DETECTION:
  Random H distribution: [1, 45], mode near E[H]=22
  Manipulated: [1, 45]
  Skill-based: [1, 45]

  Random 5th percentile: H = 5
  Random 95th percentile: H = 43
  Manipulated below 5th: 10.0%
  Skill-based below 5th: 13.2%

CONCLUSION:
  Both manipulated and skill-based produce LOW H (strong hierarchy).
  H alone CANNOT distinguish manipulation from real skill.
  But H can detect that SOME structure exists (H << E[H]).
  To distinguish manipulation from skill: need the SPECTRAL
  decomposition (which Fourier levels deviate from random).
